  "verse": 2,
  "plain_english": "Dyczkowski-mode.*\n\n**The homage that is two-resting, and who is worthy to make it**\n\nThe work opens with a prostration — and Abhinavagupta takes care to say what that prostration is. It\nis not a bending of the body, nor an empty sound. It is a *two-resting* homage: it rests at once in the\nLord, whom it exalts, and in the devotee, whom it subordinates. The words of homage — \"jaya,\" \"victory,\"\n\"namaḥ,\" \"I bow,\" \"I worship\" — all move by one of these two directions, but each must culminate in the\nother. The \"victory\" of the Lord, to be real, requires the devotee's self-subordination; the \"I bow\" of\nthe devotee, to be real, must rest in the Lord's excess. The two — the exalting and the subordinating —\nare one act, two aspects, made manifest by the single word of homage before the whole assembly.\n\nAnd the attaining of the Lord, the commentary tells us, is the attainment of *dāsya* — of\nservant-ness. And the servant-ness is extremely hard to get: that is what \"kathaṃcit,\" \"in some way,\"\nmeans. It is not a title; it is the very heart of the eligibility. And it is fruitful of the greatest\ngood — for in becoming the servant of the Lord one attains the Lord's own excess, and all the fortunes\n(dāsyalakṣmī) follow from that servant-ness, granted by the Lord's grace. It is this that makes the\ndevotee one who has attained all.\n\nThe homage, moreover, is made in the mind and the speech — the mind-awareness and the word-essence —\nnever in the inert body. \"Even by speech,\" the text says, and by the \"even\" we understand \"and by the\nbody.\" But the prostration proper is the offering of the undivided mind-and-speech, which is the\nconsciousness-power itself; and in offering that, the devotee offers the whole world to the Lord. As the\nteaching says: \"he should offer his own self.\" The physical prostration — the bowing, the eight-limbed\nsalutation — is only the upacāra, the visible mark by which the inner offering is inferred, not the\nprimary act.\n\nAnd so the question of who is worthy. The objection arises: \"the eligibility suits the explainer, who\nhas attained; but how does it suit the mere listeners?\" Abhinavagupta's answer is that the listener and\nthe explainer are not two separate undertakings: the recognition is one, and the servant-ness is one. It\nis by this that the Lord's favor is won — the recognition is preceded by, and inferred from, the Lord's\nfavor. And this is where the Gītā's four kinds of devotees come in — the distressed, the seeker of\nknowledge, the seeker of wealth, and the knower. All of them, in their several gradations, are worthy of\nthe servant-ness and of the recognition. The door is not narrow in the way of the world's ranks; it is\nnarrow only in the way of the heart — for what is required is the self-subordination, the dāsya, and\nthat, once attained, draws the Lord near.\n\n- **Preserved:** the right teacher (samyagvyutpādaka); the Buddhists as makers-and-haters (the\n  Kathāmukhatilaka); the unprecedented prostration; the two-resting homage (jaya/self-subordination,\n  namaḥ/Lord's-excess); the jar-shines-and-sees; \"kathaṃcit\" = the dāsya is hard to get; \"mahāphala\" =\n  the attainment of all fortunes (dāsyalakṣmī, by the Lord's grace); \"vācāpi\" = by body too; the\n  homage in mind/speech, not the inert body; the offering of the whole world; \"he should offer his own\n  self\"; the physical prostration as the upacāra (inferred mark); the \"satyam\" (obstacle-removal vs.\n  the greater purpose); \"adyāpi\" (un-broken, by the book); the explainer honored; the recognition\n  preceded by the Lord's favor; the listener-not-separate objection and answer; the four Gītā-devotees\n  all eligible.\n- **Added (SUPPLIED, recoverable):** \"It is not a title; it is the very heart of the eligibility\"\n  (states the dāsya); \"in offering that, the devotee offers the whole world\" (glosses the\n  viśvārpaṇa); \"the door is narrow only in the way of the heart\" (the eligibility's point).\n  Interpretive unpacking, not new doctrine.\n- **NOT resolved:** the Kathāmukhatilaka reference; the exact gradation of the four devotees (the\n  text's continuation). Logged, not invented.",
  "sanskrit_cite": "meva | dṛṣṭimanujjhan | samyagvyutpādakaḥ | śāstrakāraḥ | iti | ucyate | | | saugataprabhṛtayastu | tatkāriṇaśca | taddveṣiṇa | iti | |",
